WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.839 --> 00:00:09.272
Rachel Okafor: Hey Erin, hey Nate. How's the week going? Nate, I saw the storm track, are you guys buried?

2
00:00:10.250 --> 00:00:22.411
Nate Simmons: Buried is generous. We're at like 140 percent of normal claims volume since Sunday. I've got people doing twelve-hour shifts and the after-hours queue is the worst part.

3
00:00:23.796 --> 00:00:27.270
Rachel Okafor: Ouch. And you're still in hurricane season through November, right?

4
00:00:27.936 --> 00:00:33.447
Nate Simmons: Yeah, technically through the end of November. October is usually the worst of it for us.

5
00:00:34.606 --> 00:00:44.392
Erin Walsh: Sorry, I'm coming in hot, I just got back from that marketing analytics conference in Chicago last night. My brain is still half in a hotel ballroom.

6
00:00:45.696 --> 00:00:47.531
Rachel Okafor: Oh nice, how was it?

7
00:00:48.931 --> 00:01:00.537
Erin Walsh: Good, actually. Lots of sessions on attribution, like, which is funny because half the room is still fighting the same last-click argument we were having in 2019.

8
00:01:00.537 --> 00:01:04.835
Erin Walsh: But it was good to get out of the building.

9
00:01:06.313 --> 00:01:08.612
Rachel Okafor: Any um, sessions that were actually useful?

10
00:01:08.844 --> 00:01:18.045
Erin Walsh: One on um, offline conversion imports, which, you know, relevant. I took a bunch of notes. I'll send them your way if there's anything worth stealing.

11
00:01:18.523 --> 00:01:30.561
Rachel Okafor: Please do. Okay, so, agenda. I want to walk through Q3 numbers, then a couple of items you flagged, and Patricia said she'd join for the last ten minutes or so.

12
00:01:30.561 --> 00:01:31.337
Rachel Okafor: Sound okay?

13
00:01:32.468 --> 00:01:33.671
Nate Simmons: Works for me.

14
00:01:34.464 --> 00:01:35.264
Erin Walsh: Yep.

15
00:01:36.183 --> 00:01:48.427
Rachel Okafor: Cool. So top of the funnel first. Erin, call volume across tracking numbers was up 11 percent quarter over quarter, which tracks with the auto campaign you launched in August.

16
00:01:48.427 --> 00:01:54.549
Rachel Okafor: Google Ads offline conversions are flowing, match rate held steady. Anything surprising on your side?

17
00:01:54.853 --> 00:02:05.391
Erin Walsh: Not really, the volume's where we we expected. The one thing, um, and I've been meaning to bring this up, is the spam filtering.

18
00:02:05.391 --> 00:02:08.465
Erin Walsh: Not a huge deal but it's noisy.

19
00:02:08.978 --> 00:02:10.394
Rachel Okafor: Okay, tell me more.

20
00:02:11.631 --> 00:02:21.522
Erin Walsh: So the spam filter is catching a chunk of our after-hours roadside calls, people calling from the shoulder of the highway, and they're dropping out of my reports.

21
00:02:21.522 --> 00:02:28.940
Erin Walsh: And I only found it because one of the claims folks mentioned a call that never showed up in the dashboard.

22
00:02:29.819 --> 00:02:36.623
Rachel Okafor: Okay. Do you have a rough sense of how many, or is it more anecdotal right now?

23
00:02:36.942 --> 00:02:53.534
Erin Walsh: Anecdotal so far. I'd guess a couple dozen a month, but I don't want to quote that. Um, the pattern seems to be short calls from mobile numbers, and the audio quality is rough because, you know, highway.

24
00:02:54.813 --> 00:03:11.294
Rachel Okafor: That makes sense. Let me take that back and have the team look at how those calls are being classified, and we can look at whether there's a way to keep them out of the the spam bucket.

25
00:03:11.294 --> 00:03:16.064
Rachel Okafor: I don't want to guess at a fix on the call.

26
00:03:16.607 --> 00:03:22.700
Erin Walsh: That's all I'm asking. It's not breaking anything, it's just, my roadside numbers look soft and they're not.

27
00:03:23.105 --> 00:03:28.848
Rachel Okafor: Got it. I'll follow up by end of next week with what we find.

28
00:03:29.818 --> 00:03:30.618
Erin Walsh: Perfect.

29
00:03:31.435 --> 00:03:38.468
Rachel Okafor: Nate, let's talk contact center. Genesys routing's been stable, PreSense is firing on the inbound sales queue. How's the agent experience?

30
00:03:39.451 --> 00:03:46.291
Nate Simmons: Stable, yeah. Screen pops are landing before the agent picks up, which was the whole point. No complaints there.

31
00:03:46.513 --> 00:03:49.841
Rachel Okafor: Good. And QM, you're still scoring the sales queues?

32
00:03:50.781 --> 00:03:56.399
Nate Simmons: Sales, yes. Claims, no, and that's the thing I wanted to talk about.

33
00:03:57.111 --> 00:03:57.911
Rachel Okafor: Okay.

34
00:03:59.156 --> 00:04:09.480
Nate Simmons: So we've got QM scoring 100 percent of the sales calls against the sales scorecard, and it's been fine. But claims is a different animal.

35
00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:19.391
Nate Simmons: A claims call is somebody you know, having the worst week of their life, and the behaviors we're coaching on sales calls don't map.

36
00:04:20.824 --> 00:04:21.624
Rachel Okafor: Right.

37
00:04:21.988 --> 00:04:40.114
Nate Simmons: And I've been trying to explain this to my ops leads and I keep not getting it across. It's like, the scorecard has a section on needs discovery and a section on closing, and on a claims call there's no close.

38
00:04:40.114 --> 00:04:49.399
Nate Simmons: There's no upsell. So So the agents get dinged for stuff that doesn't apply and then they stop trusting the scores.

39
00:04:50.602 --> 00:04:54.358
Rachel Okafor: So the scorecard itself is the mismatch, not the scoring engine.

40
00:04:54.892 --> 00:05:02.983
Nate Simmons: I think so, but I don't know how to say what I actually want. It's not just swapping words on the scorecard.

41
00:05:02.983 --> 00:05:13.648
Nate Simmons: The weighting is different, the call types are different. A fender bender and a total loss are not the same conversation, and I want the scoring to know that.

42
00:05:14.836 --> 00:05:19.571
Rachel Okafor: Okay, so walk me through an example. Take a total loss call.

43
00:05:20.883 --> 00:05:33.408
Nate Simmons: Total loss, the agent's job is to absorb the emotion, be clear about next steps, not over-promise on the settlement, and get the person off the phone with a plan.

44
00:05:33.408 --> 00:05:40.922
Nate Simmons: Empathy is the whole job. Versus a policy question, which is accuracy and speed. Those are opposite scorecards.

45
00:05:42.409 --> 00:05:45.461
Rachel Okafor: And today you're scoring both against the sales scorecard.

46
00:05:45.948 --> 00:05:56.382
Nate Simmons: Yeah, which is why for claims we need to grade empathy differently, like a total loss call is not a policy question, and right now I can't build that.

47
00:05:57.796 --> 00:06:15.528
Rachel Okafor: Okay. Let me make sure I've got it. You want claims scorecards that are segmented by call type, so a total loss call gets graded on a different set of behaviors than a routine policy question, and the weighting reflects that.

48
00:06:16.917 --> 00:06:21.453
Nate Simmons: Yes. That's it. That's what I've been trying to say for three weeks.

49
00:06:22.154 --> 00:06:43.287
Rachel Okafor: Okay, I'm going to write that up and take it to the product team. I can't tell you today what's possible, but I want to get your exact example in front of them because the total loss versus policy question distinction is the clearest version of this I've heard.

50
00:06:44.445 --> 00:06:49.335
Nate Simmons: Good. And I'm happy to get on a call with them if that helps.

51
00:06:50.292 --> 00:06:52.862
Rachel Okafor: It might. Let me find out first.

52
00:06:54.172 --> 00:06:54.972
Rachel Okafor: Go ahead.

53
00:06:55.579 --> 00:07:02.542
Rachel Okafor: Okay, last thing before Patricia joins, Adobe Analytics. Anything you need from me on the integration side?

54
00:07:03.860 --> 00:07:05.665
Erin Walsh: No, it's fine. Data's flowing.

55
00:07:06.039 --> 00:07:08.228
Rachel Okafor: Great. Let me grab Patricia.

56
00:07:08.796 --> 00:07:16.038
Patricia Hale: Hi everyone, sorry, I'm two minutes late, I was on a call with our Ohio governor's office thing. What did I miss?

57
00:07:16.250 --> 00:07:21.808
Nate Simmons: Rachel's taking a couple of things back to product, mostly QM for claims.

58
00:07:22.224 --> 00:07:32.795
Patricia Hale: Good. That's the one I care about most, honestly. Our claims CSAT is fine but the coaching side has been a a gap for a while.

59
00:07:33.463 --> 00:07:40.445
Rachel Okafor: We're on it. Nate gave me a really clear example, so I have something concrete to bring to the team.

60
00:07:41.455 --> 00:07:53.985
Patricia Hale: Great. And I'll say, since I'm here, the PreSense routing is honestly the best thing we bought last year. My distribution VPs bring it up unprompted, which never happens with technology.

61
00:07:54.420 --> 00:07:58.002
Rachel Okafor: That's good to hear. The screen pops are landing clean?

62
00:07:59.356 --> 00:08:05.847
Patricia Hale: Clean. Agents know who they're talking to before they say hello. It's changed the tone of the calls.

63
00:08:07.248 --> 00:08:16.086
Rachel Okafor: Love that. Okay, so where we landed. Erin, spam classification on after-hours roadside calls, I'll come back to you by end of next week.

64
00:08:16.086 --> 00:08:23.083
Rachel Okafor: Nate, claims scorecards, I'll write it up and take it to product. Everything else is steady. Anything I'm missing?

65
00:08:23.968 --> 00:08:25.543
Nate Simmons: No, that covers it.

66
00:08:25.837 --> 00:08:26.637
Erin Walsh: Same.

67
00:08:27.958 --> 00:08:35.475
Patricia Hale: Nothing from me. Rachel, thank you for running these, they're actually useful, which is not something I say uh, about QBRs.

68
00:08:35.923 --> 00:08:41.823
Rachel Okafor: I'll take it. I'll send notes tomorrow. Nate, hang in there with the season.

69
00:08:42.297 --> 00:08:44.026
Nate Simmons: Yeah, thanks. Talk soon.

70
00:08:44.683 --> 00:08:45.553
Erin Walsh: Bye everyone.

71
00:08:46.820 --> 00:08:47.620
Patricia Hale: Take care.
